 ## Operating characteristics
 
-*   **Time Complexity:** The forward pass is $O(N \cdot M)$ where $N$ is the number of Gaussians and $M$ is the number of pixels, but optimized via tile-based rasterization to be effectively linear in practice. The backward pass is similar. Densification adds $O(N)$ overhead every few hundred iterations.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot D)$ where $D$ is the dimensionality of Gaussian attributes (position, rotation, scale, opacity, SH coefficients). For SH degree 3, $D \approx 30$. Memory usage scales linearly with the number of Gaussians, which can reach millions for high-fidelity scenes.
-*   **Measured Performance:** On standard datasets (e.g., Mip-NeRF 360), 3DGS achieves real-time rendering (>100 FPS) on modern GPUs (e.g., RTX 3090/4090) at 1080p resolution. Training time is typically 10-30 minutes per scene.
-*   **Strengths:** Real-time rendering, high visual fidelity, explicit representation allows for easy editing and compression.
-*   **Limitations:** High memory usage for large scenes, potential for "floaters" (transient artifacts), sensitivity to initialization and densification parameters.
-*   **When to use:** When real-time novel view synthesis is required and high visual quality is prioritized over memory efficiency.
-*   **When not to use:** When memory is severely constrained (consider compression methods like [Data Compression and Storage Optimization](12943-data-compression-and-storage-optimization.md)) or when the scene is extremely large (consider hierarchical methods like [Hierarchical, Graph-Based, and Adaptive Structures](17106-hierarchical-graph-based-and-adaptive-structures.md)).
+**Time Complexity:**
+*   Training: $O(N \cdot I \cdot P)$, where $N$ is number of Gaussians, $I$ is iterations, $P$ is pixels. Densification adds overhead every $K$ iterations.
+*   Rendering: $O(N \cdot T)$, where $T$ is tiles. Sorting is $O(N \log N)$ per tile.
+
+**Memory Complexity:**
+*   Storage: $O(N \cdot D)$, where $D$ is attribute size (position, scale, rotation, opacity, SH). For $D=1$, 12 floats per Gaussian.
+*   GPU Memory: Dominated by Gaussian attributes and optimizer state. Can exceed 1GB for real-world scenes [12927-compact-3d-gaussian-representation-c3dgs.md].
+
+**Measured Performance:**
+*   Standard 3DGS achieves real-time rendering (30-100+ FPS) on modern GPUs for scenes with $10^5-10^6$ Gaussians [6233-optimization-and-parallelization].
+*   CF-Splat reduces computation time by 27.5×–378.0× compared to iterative optimization [16773-closed-form-triangle-splatting-cf-splat.md].
+*   LS-Gaussian achieves 5.41× speedup over edge GPU baseline, up to 17.3× with custom accelerator [S8].
+
+**Strengths:**
+*   Real-time rendering speed.
+*   High-fidelity novel view synthesis.
+*   Explicit representation allows easy editing and compression.
+
+**Limitations:**
+*   High memory usage for large scenes.
+*   Sensitive to initialization and hyperparameters (e.g., scene extent).
+*   Struggles with transparent/refractive objects without extensions.
+
+**When to Use:**
+*   Real-time novel view synthesis.
+*   Interactive 3D editing.
+*   Scenes with complex geometry and appearance.
+
+**When Not to Use:**
+*   Scenes with extreme transparency/refraction (without extensions).
+*   Memory-constrained environments without compression/offloading.
+*   Applications requiring explicit surface meshes (without extensions).
 
 ## Background
 
-3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [S1, S3]. It revolutionized neural rendering by providing a real-time alternative to NeRFs, using explicit 3D Gaussians instead of implicit neural fields. The method builds on the concept of splatting, where 3D primitives are projected and rasterized onto the 2D image plane. The implementation details described here are based on the original paper and subsequent optimizations for training stability, efficiency, and specific use cases like text-to-3D generation [S1] and sparse-view reconstruction [S6].
+**Origin:**
+3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [6233-optimization-and-parallelization]. It builds on the concept of splatting (rasterizing 3D primitives to 2D) and differentiable rendering.
+
+**History and Development:**
+*   Early work used point clouds and neural networks for view synthesis.
+*   3DGS replaced neural fields with explicit Gaussian primitives, enabling faster training and rendering.
+*   Subsequent variants addressed limitations: compression [12943-data-compression-and-storage-optimization.md], dynamic scenes [16342-dynamic-and-spacetime-modeling.md], inverse rendering [15764-inverse-and-internal-rendering.md], etc.
+
+**Usage:**
+*   Used in computer vision, graphics, and AR/VR for novel view synthesis, 3D editing, and scene reconstruction.
+*   Integrated into libraries like `gsplat` [S3] and `tiny-cuda-nn` [S2].
 
